1. What is String in Java?
String is a class in java.lang that represents a sequence of characters.

Key Characteristics:
Immutable (cannot be changed)
Stored in String Constant Pool (SCP)
Thread-safe by nature
Frequently used in Java & Spring APIs

String s = "Java";

Interview one-liner
“String is immutable, thread-safe, and optimized using String Constant Pool.”

2. String Immutability (🔥 Very Important)
Example
public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "Java";
        s.concat(" Spring");

        System.out.println(s);
    }
}

Output
Java
👉 concat() creates a new object, original string remains unchanged.

Correct Way:
String s = "Java";
s = s.concat(" Spring");
System.out.println(s);

Output
Java Spring

3. String Constant Pool (SCP)
String s1 = "Java";
String s2 = "Java";
String s3 = new String("Java");

| Comparison      | Result |
| --------------- | ------ |
| `s1 == s2`      | true   |
| `s1 == s3`      | false  |
| `s1.equals(s3)` | true   |

👉 SCP avoids duplicate objects → memory optimization

4. Important String Methods (🔥 Interview-Critical)
  String s = "Java Spring Boot";
| Method                      | Output                   |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------ |
| `s.length()`                | 16                       |
| `s.charAt(0)`               | J                        |
| `s.substring(5)`            | Spring Boot              |
| `s.contains("Spring")`      | true                     |
| `s.replace("Java", "Core")` | Core Spring Boot         |
| `s.toUpperCase()`           | JAVA SPRING BOOT         |
| `s.split(" ")`              | ["Java","Spring","Boot"] |
| `s.trim()`                  | removes spaces           |

5. equals() vs == (🔥 MUST KNOW)
String a = "Java";
String b = new String("Java");

System.out.println(a == b);
System.out.println(a.equals(b));

Output
false
true

== → reference comparison
equals() → content comparison

